paths: file:v3/src/Parser.scala file:v3/uniml/UniFront.scala file:v3/src/Lower.scala file:v3/src/Source.scala
scope: |
  SSC3-13 slice A. `extension [A](fa: F[A]) def map[B](…)` inside a `trait` is refused by
  `Parser.scala:1539` as "a `trait` member that is not a `def`". That single limit is what stops
  `std/foldable-traversable.ssc` and `std/functor-applicative-monad.ssc` from loading, which is what
  stops the whole typeclass tower the owner wants `Dataset` abstracted over — and it is 9 refusals
  in the corpus on its own.

  MEASURED BEFORE CLAIMING: higher-kinded types are NOT the blocker. `trait Foldable[F[_]]: def
  sizeOf[A](fa: F[A]): Int` compiles and runs today.

  WIDENED 2026-08-13, BEFORE the edit and not after, exactly as this claim said it would be.
  v3's own parser now accepts the construct — but `Front.default` is UNIML when it is registered,
  so `exec`/`build` never saw the fix. I edited the front that does not compile it, which is the
  rule `ask-which-front-compiles-it-before-editing-a-front` exists for; three probes caught it.
  UniML already handles `U.Extension` at top level and lifts the receiver identically, so the
  second half is the symmetric branch in its trait-member match.

  WIDENED AGAIN 2026-08-13 to `v3/src/Lower.scala`, before the edit. The parser branches alone
  were MEASURED and REJECTED: they moved 6 corpus cases from an honest refusal to a WRONG ANSWER
  (`Cons.fmap was called but does not exist`), because an extension member became an ordinary trait
  method dispatched by the receiver's runtime tag. Parsing a construct without lowering it is worse
  than refusing it.

  The lowering half is the owner's decision of 2026-08-13, recorded in
  `v3/specs/20-core-language.md` §4a1: resolve the instance from the receiver's CONSTRUCTOR and from
  a parameter's DECLARED type, prefer the SUBTRAIT when several instances provide one method, refuse
  when the traits are unrelated.

  WIDENED A THIRD TIME to `v3/src/Source.scala`, before the edit. The last one-sided file is
  `std-index`: `Source.program` treats a `.ssc` as code IN ITS ENTIRETY when it finds no CODE fence,
  but `std/index.ssc` has a ```text fence and no scalascript one — so its markdown headings reach
  the parser and it dies on `#`. The rule should ask whether the file has ANY fence, not whether it
  has a code fence. Measured blast radius before touching it: exactly TWO files in the tree match
  that shape, `std/index.ssc` and `std/graphql.ssc`, and BOTH are refused today, so nothing that
  works can regress.

  NARROW: four files, one construct and one fence rule. v3 already has top-level extensions and `Lower.rewriteExtensionCalls`; this is
  the same construct one scope deeper, so the work is expected to be in the parser rather than the
  lowering. If it turns out to need `Lower` as well, the claim gets widened before that edit, not
  after.
